# CLAUDE.local.md — private overlay

**Gitignored.** Project-private supplement to `CLAUDE.md`. Claude Code
auto-loads this; the public repo never sees it. Backups of all
referenced docs live in `plans/` (workspace, also private).

---

## Required reading before writing performance-sensitive code

Three private docs (gitignored, backups in workspace plans):

| Doc | Trigger — read it BEFORE writing or editing... |
|---|---|
| `DOCS/STRATEGY_AND_CODING_RULES.md` | any code in hot path, slow path, OMS drainer, parsing, ML inference, strategies, RollingStats / RegimeSignals / ExecutionCore — i.e., anything in `CoreFrameworks/`, `Strategies/`, `ML_Headers/`, `DataStream/`, or `FixedPoint/`. The doc is 11 strict invariants (no malloc, no virtual, no mutex, no sleep_for, no atof/strstr in parsers, cache alignment, branchless, AVX-512-friendly data layouts, SCHED_FIFO/isolcpus deployment) — every new file in those dirs must comply |
| `DOCS/LATENCY_OPTIMIZATION_AUDIT.md` | any optimization investigation, performance-regression triage, or v5.11 sprint work. 13 parts, ~40 specific items: hot path (Part 1), slow path SIMD (2+5), arch invariants (3), ingestion+ML+memory (4), system tail variance (6), allocator eradication (7), parsing (8), OMS variance (9), concurrency (10), FPN libs (11), HFT system ops (12), hardware/OS tuning (13) |
| `plans/_cross-cutting/2026-05-06-latency-path-discipline.md` | any modification to **any code on a latency-impacting path**: `ExecutionCore_Tick` / `BG_Evaluate` / `SG_Evaluate` / `ExecutionCore` / `GateParameters` / `ParameterSlot` (hot path), `EventLoop_RebuildOneCore` / `RollingStats_Push` / `Regime_ComputeSignals` (slow path), `OMS_DrainSubmit` / `OrderManager_Tick` (OMS drainer), producer fan-out, snapshot publish, parsing. **7 architectural rules** scaled by cadence (hot = all; slow = most; OMS = subset) + an **anti-pattern history**, distilled from v5.10/v5.11 audit cycles: cache-line layout discipline (FPN<64>=24B!), no-I/O-on-failure pattern (counter not fprintf), SPSC ring failure handling, branch predictor patterns, FPN sizing awareness, dense flat arrays only, cross-thread sync patterns. Plus a **pre-merge verification checklist**. |

**Workflow when about to edit one of those areas:** open the rules doc
first, scan the relevant rule sections, then write the code. The audit
doc is consulted when the rule is "yes but how" — it has concrete
implementation suggestions per-site (with file:line references). The
hot-path discipline doc is the third leg — read it specifically when
touching hot-path layout / per-tick code, since it captures patterns
the rules doc misses (FPN sizing math, failure-path observability,
cache-line-span analysis).

## Going-forward rule: suggest mid-sprint audits when work impacts downstream plans (set 2026-05-10)

**When a ship's work has IMPACT on the rest of the planned sub-ships OR the next plan, SUGGEST a relevant audit; wait for operator greenlight or operator-initiated. Don't auto-trigger.**

**Trigger criteria — "impact" = any of:**
- HIGH-RISK ship just shipped (HMAC-chain-preservation work, hot-path migrations, structural refactors that downstream ships build on)
- New pattern field-tested for the first time (subsequent ships will follow the pattern; audit verifies it's sound before propagation)
- Plan amendments may be needed before next ship if findings emerge
- Cross-cutting changes (registry extensions, X-macro changes that affect multiple files) where downstream ships inherit the structure
- Operator explicitly invites checks ("also run new checks if you need to or think its a good idea")

**When NOT to suggest:**
- Routine pattern-application ships (subsequent applications of a field-tested pattern; e.g., .F.1 after .F validated; .F.3 after .F.1 + .F.2 validated)
- Pure additive work (new tests, new docs, comments)
- Bug fixes with bounded scope

**Suggestion format:** present the audit suggestion + recommended subagent dispatch (e.g., "/test-strength-audit + /dod-audit in parallel on commit X") + 1-sentence rationale (what risk it would catch). Wait for operator response before launching.

**Why:** ensures we're not auto-triggering audits when not needed (friction), but we ARE catching issues at structural boundaries before downstream ships inherit them. Operator retains decision authority + can also bring up audits independently.

**Caramel's framing 2026-05-10:** "we just need the associated docs to make sure were using it, and not ignoring the structure and architecture laid out". Audit cadence is operator-policy + agent-suggestion when impact-triggers fire, not automated. Established after v5.14.9.F.2 HIGH-RISK ship validated the manual mid-sprint audit pattern caught real findings (HIGH.1 + HIGH.2 pre-coding; GREEN post-coding).

