## Going-forward rule: prefer boundary-stable refactors over wide cascades

When refactoring something whose outputs cross a type or struct
boundary into many consumers (RegimeSignals fields, FeatureRegistry,
GUI panels, snapshots, etc.), **default to keeping the public boundary
types unchanged and doing the new computation INSIDE the function**.

This generalizes the audit's "Reducing Memory Touch Sites" (Part 3)
from cache lines to source files: don't sprawl a refactor across many
sites if you can isolate it behind a stable interface. Achieve the
goal (bytewise determinism, faster math, better precision, etc.) via
deterministic conversions at the edges, not rewrites of every consumer.

**Worked example (v5.10.0b.2.5.C):** FlowFeatures conversion to
FPN_Exp/FPN_Sqrt could have cascaded `FlowState → RegimeSignals →
FeatureRegistry → GUI → snapshot` (6 files, 3 hours, big merge risk).
Instead: keep `double` at the function boundaries, do FPN math
internally, return double via `FPN_ToDouble` at the edges. Saves the
cascade for v5.11 where there's dedicated budget. Same determinism
contract, much smaller blast radius.

**When you spot a refactor in a future session:**
- Count files in the cascade — 1-3 OK, 4+ stop and propose stable
  boundary first
- If the boundary type itself is the gap (e.g. operator wants the
  RegimeSignals fields to be FPN), then cascade is correct — but
  call this out to the operator explicitly so the choice is
  intentional rather than absorbed into "let's refactor everything"
- Performance-sensitive boundaries (hot path) may need cascade to
  avoid double work; weigh that against blast radius

This rule applies to Claude AND Gemini sessions; the operator
formalized it 2026-05-06 after the FlowFeatures scope discussion.
Memory file: `memory/feedback_reduce_touch_sites.md`.

## Going-forward rule for new plans (cold-pickup completeness)

When writing a new plan in `plans/`, include these 10 context fields
so a fresh session 7+ days later can pick it up without chat memory.
The `/readiness` skill enforces this via its Cold-pickup section.

1. **Branch state** — name the SPECIFIC branch (or "stay on existing
   branch with rollback tags"). Don't leave it as "TBD" or "create
   new branch" if operator policy is to stay on the sprint branch.
2. **Phase execution order matches dependency order** — if phase B
   depends on phase C, list C before B. If you must keep tag
   numbering different from execution order, call it out explicitly.
3. **First concrete move per phase** — every phase has a "Step 0"
   that a fresh session can do mechanically without re-investigating
   ("Step 0: add `FPN_FromInt` constructor at FixedPointN.hpp:124"
   not "convert primitives to FPN").
4. **Function / constructor / macro names** — say `FPN_FromInt`, not
   "a pure-integer constructor". If the function doesn't exist,
   make adding it explicit Step 0 work.
5. **File:line refs for cited tests / baselines** — instead of
   "re-run the v5.9.2 replay-determinism test", say "re-run
   `tests/controller_test.cpp:10251`".
6. **Stale-claim audit** — before committing the plan, grep the
   files you claim contain X. If the codebase already has X (or
   doesn't have what you claim it has), update the plan.
7. **Effort claims reconcile with file size deltas** — if you say
   "~200 LOC convert", `wc -l` the file and confirm there's actually
   200 LOC of the relevant type. Stale large estimates leak from
   draft to draft.
8. **Source-audit references with paths** — cite the workspace plan
   / Gemini audit / KNOWN_ISSUES section by filename, not "per the
   audit".
9. **Predecessor / dependent plans named with paths** — say
   `plans/v5.10-ensemble-engine/subplans/2026-05-04-v5.10.0b-fpn-end-to-end.md`, not just "v5.10.0b".
10. **Tag names locked + rollback anchors** — each phase has a
    unique git tag name; if you expect to roll back, note the
    `pre-<tag>` anchor that exists or should be created.

When amending an EXISTING plan (e.g. via /plan-check findings),
re-walk these 10 fields against the amendment to make sure you
didn't introduce new staleness while fixing old.

## Going-forward rule for new docs

**When creating a new doc, default to private** if any of these apply:
- Captures unshipped strategy direction or roadmap (e.g. multi-symbol
  designs, future autotuning, planned strategy variants)
- Distills optimization findings, profiling output, or hardware-tuned
  performance work
- Names operator-specific deployment details (NUMA topology, NIC
  brand, isolcpus core IDs, etc.)
- References private cfg values, real account balances, real symbols
  beyond the public BTCUSDT example

**Where to write it:**
- **Private edge doc** → write directly into `plans/` (symlinked to
  workspace, gitignored). Date-prefix the filename (e.g.
  `plans/YYYY-MM-DD-thing.md`).
- **Public architectural doc** → write into `DOCS/`. Architectural =
  invariants, parity rules, code map, function interface contracts,
  build instructions, public testing recipes, post-incident learnings
  that are bug-class-level (not operator-edge-level).

**Auto-private gitignore patterns** (already in `.gitignore`):
- `*_AUDIT.md` — optimization audits, latency findings
- `FUTURE_*.md` — roadmap / planned-strategy direction
- `*-design-notes.md` — versioned design idea backlogs
- `*-suggestions.md` — HFT / optimization research outputs
- `*-hft-*.md` — HFT-flavored notes
- `*-OPTIMIZATION-*.md` — surgical optimization references
- `*-plan-check.md` — agent-generated plan-check reports (canonical home is `plans/plan_checks/` per skill convention)
- `STRATEGY_AND_CODING_RULES.md` — Gemini-distilled coding invariants
- `GEMINI.md`, `GEMINI.local.md` — Gemini agent's project memory file (analog of CLAUDE.local.md)
- `CLAUDE.local.md`, `*.local.md` — project-private memory overlays

If you write a file matching one of those patterns into `DOCS/` or
the repo root, it auto-stays-private. But still prefer `plans/` for
new private content — single source of truth, automatic workspace
backup, doesn't dual-track files.

**If unsure: ask the user.** "Should this doc be private?" is cheaper
than untracking + history-rewriting later.

## Going-forward rule for workspace backup (set 2026-05-06)

**Anything gitignored at the engine repo, outside of pure
data/build/runtime artifacts, gets backed up to the workspace.**
Workspace = `~/code/tick-trader-percore-workspace`, pushed to
`Jennyfirrr/tick-trader-percore-workspace.git` (private remote).

The `/sync-workspace` skill mirrors:
- `plans/`, `.claude/skills/` — auto-propagate via symlinks (no copy needed)
- `engine.cfg`, `backtest.cfg`, `controller.cfg`, `secrets.cfg` → `workspace/configs/` (explicit copy by skill)
- `.env`, `.env.*` → `workspace/configs/` (explicit copy by skill)
- `CLAUDE.local.md`, `*.local.md` → `workspace/<name>.backup` (explicit copy by skill)

**NOT mirrored** (data / runtime / regenerable):
- Build dirs, vendor/, bin/, .cache/, .clangd/, compile_commands.json
- Logs, paper_runs/, baseline_run/, data/, *_metrics.csv, *_order_history.csv
- ImGui state files, IDE configs, session scratch (continue.md)
- `models/` — handled separately if operator chooses

**When you add a new gitignore category** (e.g. a new audit-style
file pattern), check if it's edge content vs runtime data:
- Edge content (research, design, optimization findings, configs) →
  also update `/sync-workspace` skill's mirror table to include it
- Runtime/data → leave gitignored, no backup needed

Run `/sync-workspace` periodically (end of session, after a plan
finalized, after a skill updated) to push the backup. The skill is
on-demand — no auto-cron — so you control the cadence.

## Known landmine: XGBoost + libgomp + pthread parallelism (set 2026-05-07, v5.11.45)

**Symptom:** `foxml_suite` SIGSEGV when clicking Train Multi-Horizon (or
Train Model post-v5.11.44 since Train Model now routes through MH
worker). Backtrace shows crash inside `libgomp::GOMP_parallel` called
from XGBoost internals — `RowsWiseBuildHistKernel`, `BuildHistLeftRight`,
`PredValueByOneTree`, `PredictDMatrix`. Hits during fold training in
WF or during prediction.

**Root cause:** XGBoost is not safe under "concurrent booster trainings
in different pthreads". libgomp's parallel-region setup races even when
each pthread calls `omp_set_num_threads(1)` to cap its OpenMP team. The
team allocation in libgomp uses shared process-global state that
multiple pthreads can corrupt simultaneously.

**Why the obvious fix didn't work:**
- v5.11.41.C: parallel multi-horizon spawned N pthreads, each pinned
  XGBoost's `xgb_train_nthread = 1`. Insufficient.
- v5.11.44 hotfix: added `omp_set_num_threads(1)` + `omp_set_dynamic(0)`
  at top of `mh_per_horizon_parallel_worker`, also pinned
  `xgb_eval_nthread = 1` (WF folds use eval not train). Still segfaulted.
- The libgomp parallel-region setup itself is the issue, not the
  thread count.

**Current mitigation (v5.11.45):** default
`cfg.multi_horizon_max_threads = 1` (forced serial). Operator opts in
to parallel mode by setting `>= 2`; worker fires a CRITICAL log
warning telling them about the segfault risk.

**Proper fix (deferred):** call
`setenv("OMP_NUM_THREADS", "1", /*overwrite=*/1)` at process start in
`foxml_suite.cpp:main()` BEFORE any pthreads exist. Sets libgomp's
global default once; all child threads inherit. No per-thread races.
Affects ALL XGBoost in the binary (not just multi-horizon), so it's
more invasive than the cfg toggle. Worth doing eventually but needs
testing across all training/prediction surfaces.

**Workaround for operator who wants speedup:** train models for
different horizons in SEPARATE foxml_suite invocations (each its own
process, no pthread interaction). E.g. shell-script that loops over
horizons, each runs `./bin/foxml_suite --batch ...` (no live UI).
Cumbersome but parity-safe.

**If a future Claude session sees a segfault in XGBoost training:**
- Check if `cfg.multi_horizon_max_threads >= 2` is set (operator opted
  in to parallel mode). If yes: set it to 1, retry serial.
- If still crashing under serial: different root cause (NOT this
  landmine). Look for actual memory bug in our worker code.

Reference:
- v5.11.41.C added the parallelism (`mh_per_horizon_parallel_worker`)
- v5.11.44 attempted libgomp fix (didn't work)
- v5.11.45 changed default to serial
- No upstream XGBoost issue tracking this specifically; it's a known
  general issue with libgomp's pthread interaction.

## Going-forward rule: codify design principles in CLAUDE.md as patterns mature (set 2026-05-09)

When a session develops a reusable architectural pattern (e.g., bit-packed
flags, AUTOPOPULATE companion, PRE/POST registry split, type-trait dispatch
via templated helpers), promote it to a CLAUDE.md item once the pattern has:
- 2+ applications in the codebase, OR
- Documented as a `DESIGN_SPECS/<pattern>.md` in workspace, AND
- Pattern applies broadly (not single-instance edge case)

Keeps the always-loaded CLAUDE.md current with how the codebase actually
thinks. Items are short (3-15 sentences with optional code snippet);
DESIGN_SPECS docs are the deep-dive (200-400 lines per pattern).

**v5.14.8 codification (2026-05-09):** items 19-23 added:
- 19: Structural fix preferred when bug class can recur (was already in
